      "value": "I have taken a look at your world using Unity's Profiler and it appears that static batching is working properly. Running the profiler with a development client shows ~2500 Draw Calls and ~1300 Batches.\n\nI haven't used IntelGPA before but what you are likely seeing is due to an implementation detail of Unity's static batching. Batches do not correspond to a single Draw Call. Unity groups geometry using the same material into combined meshes. These combined meshes contain a sub-mesh for each of the original meshes. Unity still uses the original meshes for frustum and occlusion culling, and only draws the sub-meshes if the corresponding original mesh is visible. The render thread draws each visible sub-mesh with a separate Draw Call. This reduces the CPU cost even though the number of Draw Calls is the same because the Draw Calls to draw different sub-meshes of the same mesh are cheaper than draw calls to draw different meshes.\n\nThis causes third-party tools like IntelGPA and RenderDoc to show more Draw Calls than Unity shows batches. This is also the case in VR with Single Pass Stereo where the left and right eyes are drawn with separate draw calls, but the right eye draw call is much cheaper than the left because it's exactly the same as the left except with the unity_StereoEyeIndex property changed.\n\nI noticed that you're using Motion Blur, this is strongly discouraged in VR. Also I noticed that you don't have your post processing volumes on a separate layer. You need to create a separate \"PostProcessing\" layer, set it as the layer on the Post-processing Layer\" script and put your volumes on that layer. Using the \"Default\" layer wastes CPU time when processing volumes. The \"Post-processing Layer\" script will show a warning until you fix this.\n\nYour performance issues appear to be a GPU bottleneck, I see a significant performance boost when I put VRChat into windowed mode (Alt+Enter) and reduce the window size. The \"Silent/Clear Water\" shader appears to be using an unnamed GrabPass which causes each mesh using it to capture its own GrabTexture. This is very expensive, so it may be causing your performance issues. You can edit the shader to share a GrabPass by giving the GrabTexture a name as shown here. https://docs.unity3d.com/Manual/SL-GrabPass.html",
      "viewerReactions": null
    },
